//? Title: Two-Scale VQ-VAE: Coarse Conditioning and Discrete-Only Decoding
//? Subtitle: Independent dictionaries, explicit surrogate gradients and fitting-only code priors
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
One code lattice can force every image detail through the same spatial scale. A two-scale
bottleneck lets coarse codes condition fine encoding while both discrete grids determine
reconstruction. This report fits a compact hierarchical VQ model on 210 local binary shapes,
with 42 validation and 42 test cases and all 21 Adam updates visible in C++. Epoch 3 wins
validation reconstruction NLL. Test MSE 0.038228 improves on the fitting-only mean's 0.048548;
9/16 coarse and 13/16 fine codes occur in fitting data. Counterfactual code controls and
unfiltered prior images distinguish mechanism checks from unsupported semantic/quality claims.
)
\toc

> Coarse codes condition fine encoding; both grids enter decoding

[Razavi, van den Oord and Vinyals (2019)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.00446)
organize vector-quantized latents hierarchically and fit code priors after learning the
encoder/decoder. This small teaching variant has a 7×7 coarse lattice and a 14×14 fine
lattice, each using its own 16×4 dictionary. Decoded coarse vectors condition the fine
encoder and final decoder. All image-dependent information reaches decoding through
quantized vectors; there is no continuous image-feature bypass to the decoder.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/vq2_vae_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram          d(51, 1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle st;
  st.width     = 5.6;
  st.height    = 2.8;
  st.font_size = 19;
  d.node("x", {0, 0}, "Binary image\n28 x 28 x 1", st)
      .node("h", {7, 0}, "Conv3 to4 / ReLU / avg2\nFine feature14 x 14 x 4", st)
      .node("e", {14, 0}, "Conv3 to4 / ReLU / avg2\nCoarse vectors7 x 7 x 4", st)
      .node("q", {21, 0}, "Coarse quantization\nIndependent book16 x 4", st)
      .node("t", {21, -6}, "Coarse-code feature\nRepeat2 / Conv3 to4 / ReLU", st)
      .node("b", {7, -6}, "Concat fine + coarse\nLinear Conv1 to4", st)
      .node("qb", {7, -12}, "Fine quantization\nIndependent book16 x 4", st)
      .node("d", {28, -12}, "Concat BOTH code paths\nConv3 to8 / ReLU / repeat2", st)
      .node("o", {35, -12}, "Linear Conv3 to1 / sigmoid\nBernoulli probabilities", st)
      .edge("x", "h")
      .edge("h", "e")
      .edge("e", "q")
      .edge("q", "t")
      .edge("h", "b")
      .edge("t", "b")
      .edge("b", "qb")
      .edge("qb", "d")
      .edge("t", "d")
      .edge("d", "o")
      .text(
          {19, -19},
          "Reconstruction skips BOTH dictionaries in reverse mode.\nFine encoder losses reach the "
          "coarse encoder through its surrogate.\nGeneration supplies two ID grids; no image or "
          "continuous encoder feature enters.",
          20)
      .save_svg("build/vq2_vae_method.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/vq2_vae_method.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Coarse code-derived features have two roles: condition fine encoding and join fine quantized vectors during decoding. Only code-derived paths reach the decoder.)
\alttext(A binary image becomes fine features and coarse quantized vectors. Coarse-derived features condition a projected fine encoding and the final decoder. Independently quantized fine vectors also enter that decoder.)

The dictionaries use nearest squared-distance assignment, with lowest-index ties. At each
level q is the selected vector, and s=stop(q)+(e-stop(e)) preserves its forward value with
an identity surrogate Jacobian to the encoder. Dictionary and commitment penalties detach
opposite targets. For levels t and b, the fitted objective is

$$
L=\operatorname{mean}_{images}R+
(D_t+D_b)+0.25(C_t+C_b),\\
R_i=\sum_{j=1}^{784}[\operatorname{softplus}(a_{ij})-x_{ij}a_{ij}],\quad
D_\ell=\operatorname{mean}_{vectors,coordinates}[\operatorname{stop}(e_\ell)-q_\ell]^2,\quad
C_\ell=\operatorname{mean}_{vectors,coordinates}[e_\ell-\operatorname{stop}(q_\ell)]^2.
$$
\alttext(The loss averages pixel-summed Bernoulli NLL over images, adds two dictionary penalties and quarter-weights two commitment penalties. Each latent penalty averages its own level's vectors and coordinates.)

Both reconstruction-to-book derivatives stop. Fine losses can still reach the coarse encoder
through its code-derived conditioning feature. The surrogate is not the derivative of hard
argmin; analytic routing checks establish that path, while continuous decoder derivatives
can be checked by finite differences away from ReLU kinks. There is no EMA dictionary update,
reset, Gaussian KL or hidden optimizer. The paper uses EMA dictionaries and squared pixel
loss; this migrated compact model uses gradient dictionary updates and Bernoulli NLL.
It does not reproduce the paper's large residual networks or neural priors.

For general image area HW, dictionary width K, vector width D and final hidden width C,
distance work is O(BHWKD), with O(BHWK) one-hot storage. Convolutions cost
O(BHW×9(D²+DC+C)); the fine lattice contains four times as many vectors as the coarse one.

> A conditional prior small enough to check by hand

A separately fitted fine-code prior uses only the aligned coarse parent and previous fine
symbol. In the worked example, coarse codes [0,1] describe a 1×2 lattice and fine codes are
[[0,0,1,1],[0,0,1,1]]. Each coarse symbol owns a 2×2 fine block, as the diagram shows.
BOS=K resets at the start of each image, not each raster row. With half-count smoothing,
parent0/BOS predicts code0 with probability 0.75; parent0/previous0 predicts it with 5/6.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/vq2_vae_parent_alignment.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n       = dm::nn;
  const auto top    = n::from_vector(std::vector<int>{0, 1}, {1, 2}),
             bottom = n::from_vector(std::vector<int>{0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 1, 1}, {1, 8});
  const n::models::Vq2ConditionalPrior prior(top, bottom, 1, 2, 2);
  const auto                           p = prior.probabilities();
  if (prior.counts().to_vector<int>() != std::vector<int>{2, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 0, 2, 0, 2, 0, 0})
    return 1;
  std::print("| parent code | previous fine | P(code0) | P(code1) |\n|---:|---|---:|---:|\n");
  for (int parent = 0; parent < 2; ++parent)
    for (int previous = 0; previous < 3; ++previous)
      std::print(
          "| {} | {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} |\n",
          parent,
          previous == 2 ? "BOS" : std::to_string(previous),
          p.at({parent, previous, 0}),
          p.at({parent, previous, 1}));
  std::print("Conditional NLL {:.9f} nats/grid.\n", prior.Nll(top, bottom));
  if (!n::equal_bits(prior.Sample(top, n::full({1, 8}, .2, n::DType::Float64)), bottom))
    return 1;
  dm::graphics::Diagram          d(40, 1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle a;
  a.width     = 3.5;
  a.height    = 2.2;
  a.font_size = 20;
  a.fill      = dm::RGB{219, 234, 254};
  auto b      = a;
  b.fill      = dm::RGB{255, 237, 213};
  d.node("t0", {2, 6}, "Coarse code0", a).node("t1", {10, 6}, "Coarse code1", b);
  for (int row = 0; row < 2; ++row)
    for (int col = 0; col < 4; ++col) {
      const int parent = col / 2;
      d.node(
          "f" + std::to_string(row * 4 + col),
          {col * 4., -row * 3.},
          "Fine code" + std::to_string(parent) + "\nParent" + std::to_string(parent),
          parent == 0 ? a : b);
    }
  d.text(
       {6, -7},
       "Fine raster order crosses the row boundary without resetting previous.\nBOS resets only at "
       "the next image; aligned parent uses floor(row/2), floor(col/2).",
       20)
      .save_svg("build/vq2_vae_parent_alignment.svg");
}
```
