> Counterfactual codes check both decoder inputs

The next program restores only six decoder leaves and supplies both codebooks as saved
lookup tensors. Rows fix coarse IDs to all-zero/all-one, columns fix fine IDs to all-zero/
all-one. These are deliberately extreme controls, not prior draws or quality-ranked images.
The original maximum probability effects of changing coarse or fine IDs from zero are
0.056142464 and 0.164884523. A nonzero effect establishes dependence, not a semantic role.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/vq2_vae_code_controls.png", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  n::NoGradGuard guard;
  const auto     arrays = n::load_npz("build/vq2_vae/model_arrays.npz");
  auto           find   = [&](const std::string& name) {
    for (const auto& [key, value] : arrays)
      if (key == name)
        return value;
    throw std::runtime_error(name);
  };
  n::ParameterStore p(2801);
  auto              warm = n::models::Vq2TopDecoder(p, n::zeros({49, 4}));
  n::models::Vq2Decoder(p, n::zeros({196, 4}), warm);
  if (p.size() != 6)
    return 1;
  for (const auto& name : p.names())
    p.set(name, find(name));
  std::vector<n::Tensor> images;
  for (int top = 0; top < 2; ++top)
    for (int bottom = 0; bottom < 2; ++bottom) {
      const auto t = n::models::VqLookup(
                     n::full({1, 49}, top, n::DType::Int32), find("top_codebook")),
                 b = n::models::VqLookup(
                     n::full({1, 196}, bottom, n::DType::Int32), find("bottom_codebook"));
      images.push_back(n::sigmoid(n::models::Vq2Decoder(p, b, n::models::Vq2TopDecoder(p, t))));
    }
  std::print(
      "Maximum coarse effect {:.9f}; fine effect {:.9f}.\n",
      n::max(n::abs(images[2] - images[0])).item(),
      n::max(n::abs(images[1] - images[0])).item());
  n::save_image_grid(
      n::concat(images, 0).reshape({4, 28, 28, 1}),
      "build/vq2_vae_code_controls.png",
      2,
      0,
      1,
      4,
      220. / 255.,
      6);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Maximum coarse effect 0.056142464; fine effect 0.164884523.
\image(figures/vq2_vae_code_controls.png)
// result_end

\caption(Rows set coarse IDs to 0/1; columns set fine IDs to 0/1. Both discrete groups influence the target-free decoder, but these synthetic code combinations have no quality guarantee.)
\alttext(Four decoder probability images compare all-zero and all-one code assignments at both levels, using a shared grayscale.)

Fine encoding itself also changes with a supplied coarse grid. The saved intervention uses
one fixed held-out image with all-zero versus all-one top assignments. The maximum fine
encoded-vector change is 0.122590229. The following program recomputes both interventions
through the native override API and verifies the saved vectors/IDs before rendering.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/vq2_vae_top_conditioning.png", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  n::NoGradGuard guard;
  const auto     arrays = n::load_npz("build/vq2_vae/model_arrays.npz"),
             saved      = n::load_npz("build/vq2_vae/top_conditioning_intervention.npz");
  auto find             = [](const n::NamedTensors& a, const std::string& name) {
    for (const auto& [key, value] : a)
      if (key == name)
        return value;
    throw std::runtime_error(name);
  };
  n::ParameterStore p(2801);
  n::models::VectorQuantizedAutoencoder2(p, n::zeros({1, 28, 28, 1}));
  for (const auto& name : p.names())
    p.set(name, find(arrays, name));
  std::vector<n::Tensor> panels{find(saved, "images")};
  for (int value = 0; value < 2; ++value) {
    const auto o = n::models::VectorQuantizedAutoencoder2(
        p, find(saved, "images"), n::full({1, 49}, value, n::DType::Int32));
    const std::string prefix = value == 0 ? "zero" : "one";
    if (!n::equal_bits(o.encoded_bottom, find(saved, prefix + "_top_encoded_bottom")) ||
        !n::equal_bits(o.bottom.indices, find(saved, prefix + "_top_bottom_indices")))
      return 1;
    panels.push_back(
        n::upsample_nearest(
            n::cast(o.bottom.indices.reshape({1, 14, 14, 1}), n::DType::Float32) / 15., 2));
  }
  n::save_image_grid(
      n::concat(panels, 0), "build/vq2_vae_top_conditioning.png", 3, 0, 1, 4, 220. / 255., 5);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/vq2_vae_top_conditioning.png)
// result_end

\caption(Columns are the fixed target image, fine ID grid under all-zero top codes and fine ID grid under all-one top codes. ID brightness is categorical display only; this is an encoding intervention, not prior generation.)
\alttext(The same binary target accompanies two fine code-ID maps produced under different forced coarse codes, revealing the top-conditioned fine encoding.)

> Generate the coarse grid, then the conditional fine grid

After network selection, fit p(top) on fit coarse grids and p(fine|top) on paired fit grids.
For generation, draw the forty-nine top symbols first using seed 2827, then the 196 fine
symbols using seed 2833 and aligned parents. Both explicit banks use Float64 MT19937
midpoint uniforms. The following code fits only saved fitting codes, verifies all counts,
probabilities, uniforms and sampled IDs, restores only decoder leaves, then checks every
saved prior probability. No target, encoder or label enters generation; all twenty draws appear.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/vq2_vae_priors.png", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  n::NoGradGuard guard;
  const auto     arrays = n::load_npz("build/vq2_vae/model_arrays.npz"),
             out        = n::load_npz("build/vq2_vae/outputs.npz"),
             saved      = n::load_npz("build/vq2_vae/prior.npz");
  auto find             = [](const n::NamedTensors& a, const std::string& name) {
    for (const auto& [key, value] : a)
      if (key == name)
        return value;
    throw std::runtime_error(name);
  };
  const n::models::VqMarkovPrior       top_prior(find(out, "fit_top_index_grid"));
  const n::models::Vq2ConditionalPrior bottom_prior(
      find(out, "fit_top_index_grid"), find(out, "fit_bottom_index_grid"));
  const auto tu = n::models::MidpointUniformNoise({20, 49}, 2827),
             bu = n::models::MidpointUniformNoise({20, 196}, 2833), top = top_prior.Sample(tu),
             bottom = bottom_prior.Sample(top, bu);
  for (const auto& [name, value] : n::NamedTensors{
           {"top_counts", top_prior.counts()},
           {"top_probabilities", top_prior.probabilities()},
           {"bottom_counts", bottom_prior.counts()},
           {"bottom_probabilities", bottom_prior.probabilities()},
           {"top_uniform", tu},
           {"bottom_uniform", bu},
           {"sample_top_ids", top},
           {"sample_bottom_ids", bottom}})
    if (!n::equal_bits(value, find(saved, name)))
      return 1;
  n::ParameterStore p(2801);
  auto              warm = n::models::Vq2TopDecoder(p, n::zeros({49, 4}));
  n::models::Vq2Decoder(p, n::zeros({196, 4}), warm);
  for (const auto& name : p.names())
    p.set(name, find(arrays, name));
  if (p.size() != 6)
    return 1;
  const auto feature =
      n::models::Vq2TopDecoder(p, n::models::VqLookup(top, find(arrays, "top_codebook")));
  const auto probability = n::sigmoid(
      n::models::Vq2Decoder(
          p, n::models::VqLookup(bottom, find(arrays, "bottom_codebook")), feature));
  if (!n::equal_bits(probability, find(saved, "probability")))
    return 1;
  n::save_image_grid(
      probability.reshape({20, 28, 28, 1}), "build/vq2_vae_priors.png", 5, 0, 1, 4, 220. / 255., 4);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/vq2_vae_priors.png)
// result_end

\caption(All twenty unfiltered hierarchical-prior decoder probabilities in draw order, five per row. The simple prior and short fit can produce weak or ambiguous shapes.)
\alttext(Twenty probability images from coarse-then-fine code generation are displayed without rejection or selection.)

Test coarse prior NLL is 27.295038556 and conditional fine NLL 75.616507032, totalling
102.911545588 nats per paired grid. These are code-grid scores, not image log likelihoods.
The raw fixed-width symbols need (49+196)×4=980 bits, excluding dictionaries, decoder, prior
tables, framing and any entropy coder. This arithmetic does not establish a codec or
compression advantage. A comparison with the smaller single-scale example would also
change parameter count and code capacity; it is not a matched architectural experiment.

> Evidence and limits

The native API owns both code-derived decoder stages, hierarchical inference and copied
conditional prior state, reusing the canonical quantizer and coarse Markov prior. The caller
owns updates, selection and explicit draws. The optional independent NumPy development check
verifies all 1,157 first parameter derivatives, both reconstruction-to-book stops, 6,272 hierarchy
derivatives, the exact first Adam step, all saved forwards, all three eligible validation
criteria, complete batch order and both prior stages. It never fits or prepares data:

```
python3 scripts/check_vector_quantized_autoencoder_2.py docs/mepml/examples/nn/2_autoencoders/2.2_variational_autoencoders/build/vq2_vae
```

The tiny run demonstrates two-scale discrete dependence and a modest MSE improvement over a
constant fitting mean. Neither that score, the code interventions nor pooled perplexity
establish semantic coarse/fine separation, realistic generation or benchmark performance.
